---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Tune from "../components/Tune.astro";

// Charity not chosen yet. Set these once the family decides.
const CHARITY = { name: "", url: "" };
// The photo-essay version of this page (full-bleed photos, scroll scenes) is kept in src/drafts/photo-essay.astro.
---
<Base title="Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns, 1929 to 2026">
  <Nav current="obituary" />
  <Tune />

  <header class="cover plain">
    <div class="cover-title col">
      <span class="lady" data-reveal>Lady</span>
      <h1 data-split>Margaret</h1>
      <span class="fullname" data-reveal>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · 1929 – 2026</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <div class="col prose">
      <p class="first">Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns died peacefully in Montreal on November 5, 2026, surrounded by her family. She was 97.</p>
      <p>To meet Margaret in her prime (which lasted, by most estimates, at least sixty years) was to meet a force. A glamorous redhead with an aristocratic air, she could fill a room before she had properly entered it. Margaret was eloquent and endlessly sociable, quick to laugh and occasionally quicker to anger, with a Scottish wit as sharp as her opinions. She loved beautiful things, stimulating conversation, and the pleasure of making an impression. Blessed with a beautiful singing voice—like her niece, the Scottish pop star Lulu—she was rarely far from a song, and her singing filled the house even through her final days.</p>
      <p>To those who knew her, Margaret appeared a woman of impeccable pedigree. Few could have guessed where she began, or the sheer distance she had travelled to become the woman they knew. She had not inherited her place in that world. She had made one for herself.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p class="dropcap">Margaret’s story began in 1929 in Glasgow’s Garngad neighborhood, an Irish Catholic enclave of soot-blackened tenements so notorious for its poverty that the city would eventually demolish much of it and give the district an entirely new name.</p>
      <p>She was one of seven children. Her father, Hugh Cairns, was a hard man who drank heavily; a prominent scar across his face and his frequent spells in prison hinted at ties to one of Glasgow’s infamous razor gangs. Her mother, Helen Darling Kennedy, died at 31 of an infection that might be readily treated today. Margaret was only five.</p>
      <p>The children were left largely to raise one another, scraping together whatever they could to eat. Then came the war, and German bombs over Glasgow. It says something about the bleakness of her upbringing that even the Blitz brought an unlikely reprieve: evacuation to the Scottish countryside, where Margaret and her sister Eleanor discovered a world of beauty far removed from the squalor of the Garngad.</p>
      <p>Margaret came from a line of remarkable—and remarkably headstrong—women. Her maternal grandmother, Helen Orr Kennedy, had risen to become the first Worthy Grand Mistress in Scotland’s Orange Order. The Protestant organization staunchly opposed marriage to Catholics, and her daughter Helen’s insistence on marrying Hugh, of Irish Catholic descent, caused a scandal on both sides. The families kept their distance, but young Margaret was not inclined to respect the divide: after her mother died, she ran away again and again to her grandmother’s house—a beautiful, well-appointed home that must have seemed palatial by comparison.</p>
      <p>These small acts of rebellion offered an early glimpse of the determination that would define her life. If a better world existed beyond the lines drawn around her, Margaret would find her way across them.</p>
      <p>After the war, Margaret entered a convent, intending to become a nun. For a poor Catholic girl in Glasgow, it offered what little else could: an education, a roof over her head, a respectable way out. But a life of quiet obedience and plain walls was never going to hold a girl who had already glimpsed beauty and wanted more of it.</p>
      <p>At nineteen, she did take vows—just not the ones intended. She married Henry Bamber, a Canadian of Irish and Scottish parentage whom she had known only briefly amid the postwar celebrations, and crossed the Atlantic with him to begin a new life in Montreal. It put an ocean between her and the brothers and sisters with whom she had survived her childhood. She would return only a handful of times.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p>Through her twenties, Margaret bore and raised three children within that marriage, in a new country, isolated from anyone who had known her before she was Mrs. Bamber. Her husband had suffered a brain injury and was, by every account, a difficult man. She had grown up in the shadow of a troubled father, and now she was living with another—and this time there was no grandmother’s house to run to. In the late 1950s, raised Catholic, with three small children and no family within three thousand miles, she divorced him.</p>
      <p>The years that followed were assuredly the hardest of an already impossible life, yet Margaret made sure nobody knew it. Not yet thirty, she juggled multiple jobs—including night shifts cleaning downtown offices—and it was still barely enough. Determined that her children would never know the poverty she had, she rented in the affluent Westmount neighborhood at a rent she could barely afford for the sake of its schools. And she carried herself there as though she had always belonged.</p>
      <p>With the 1960s, her life turned. The decade was opening doors for women, and Margaret walked through every one she could find. At its start, she married Ted Liontos, a handsome Greek photographer, and their daughter Anthea, her youngest, was born. She began modelling—with that striking red hair and expressive features, it was easy to see why—and briefly co-owned a small boutique. The marriage lasted only a few years, and before long she was on her own again, now with four children.</p>
      <p>Through the 1960s and 1970s, she worked behind the counters of Montreal’s finest stores, among them Birks, the storied jeweller, where her natural magnetism and charisma made her a natural. Suitors were never in short supply, but any man who wanted Margaret without her children was soon shown the door—they came together, or not at all.</p>
      <p>By then, the girl from the Garngad had become a woman of Westmount: glamorous, quick-witted, and perfectly at ease among even its best-connected families. The Glasgow of her childhood was gone from her voice entirely. She spoke properly, deliberately and eloquently, with only a slight Scottish lilt left. What had begun as a performance had, somewhere along the way, simply become her. And, remarkably for a single mother of the time, Margaret eventually saved enough to buy a house of her own.</p>
      <p>She raised her daughters “never to be impressed by a man who pulled up in a Rolls-Royce.” The way to accomplish that, she reasoned, was to make such things familiar. Posture and manners were drilled into them, but more important was the conviction that no one was inherently their superior. Anthea was presented as a debutante at the St. Andrew’s Ball, a mere generation after the tenements of Glasgow.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p>In the 1970s, Margaret discovered Christian Science and began what would become a lifelong intellectual and spiritual devotion. She immersed herself in the writings of Mary Baker Eddy, drawn to a philosophy that placed spiritual reality above the apparent limitations of the material world. There was an irony in a woman so devoted to beautiful things finding her deepest convictions in a faith that questioned the very primacy of matter. Margaret saw no contradiction. She had never been particularly interested in accepting appearances as the final word on anything.</p>
      <p>Then came unspeakable grief against which neither faith nor resilience could offer easy defence. At the turn of the 1980s, Margaret lost both her son Colin and her daughter Helen barely a year apart. She had dedicated her adult life trying to spare her children the hardships she had known. There was nothing she could do to spare them this, or herself the terrible fact of surviving them.</p>
      <p>Much of the decade that followed was spent reckoning with those losses. In Bermuda, she wrote poetry and turned increasingly inward. Christian Science became an ever more central part of that search. She studied Mary Baker Eddy’s teachings with extraordinary devotion, returning again and again to questions of suffering, healing, spiritual identity and the nature of existence. These were not abstract philosophical interests to Margaret. They were questions she had lived.</p>
      <p>She never ceased to love beauty, laughter or the company of others. But beneath the formidable social presence was a turbulent inner life of reflection, sorrow and spiritual inquiry that few outside her closest family could have fully appreciated.</p>
      <p class="sep">· · ·</p>
      <p>By the mid-1990s, when Anthea was raising a young son alone while working in Manhattan, Margaret would not hear of leaving him to strangers. She knew what it meant to raise children without family nearby; her daughter would not have to. Decades after crossing an ocean to make a life for herself, she left her beloved Montreal for New York to help raise her only grandchild, Sean. She brought with her the convictions she held most dear: good manners, an appreciation for beauty, an abiding faith in Christian Science, and the quiet assurance that one need never feel out of place in any company.</p>
      <p>The years did nothing to dull her. Well into her eighties she still loved a practical joke, never more than at Christmas, when a little Tiffany box in a stocking might turn out to hold a set of false teeth or a few toenail clippings. She never lost her gift for connecting with people. Left on her own for a few hours in Positano, her family returned to find her the best of friends with a local contessa she had only just met. And when something had truly upset Anthea, she would listen patiently, let her get all of it out, and then deliver a single, unsentimental verdict: “So what?”</p>
      <p>In later life her son Paul gifted her a small plot of land in Scotland and, with it, a certificate naming her Lady Margaret. She was touched, and never entirely comfortable with the title, which struck her as fraudulent. Nobody else thought so. She had become more of a lady than most will ever meet, and had needed no certificate to do it.</p>
      <p>In her final years, age gradually took from Margaret the self-sufficiency she had fought so hard to preserve. For a woman accustomed to being the one others depended upon, accepting their care was perhaps the hardest adjustment of all. Her family remained close, grateful for the chance to return some of what she had given so freely.</p>
      <p>She was predeceased by her beloved children Colin and Helen, and by her six siblings, Eleanor (Nellie), Elizabeth (Betty), James, David (Davey), Hugh Jr. (Hughie) and Colin. She is survived by her son, Paul; her daughter, Anthea; and her grandson, Sean, who will carry her memory forward with love, laughter and gratitude.</p>
      <p>In accordance with her wishes, Margaret will be cremated, and at a private family ceremony her ashes will be scattered where she will join Colin and Helen once more.</p>
      <p>It will come as no surprise that Margaret’s wit held to the very end. When nurses explained that they needed her consent to take some CT scans, the former model had one question: “Am I being paid for these pictures?”</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col prose to-book">
      <p><a href="/memories">Share a memory of Margaret, or read what others have written, in the memory book.</a></p>
    </div>

    {CHARITY.name && (
      <section class="col prose tribute">
        <p>In lieu of flowers, the family asks that you consider a donation to {CHARITY.name}, a cause close to Margaret’s heart.</p>
        <a class="btn" href={CHARITY.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donate to {CHARITY.name}</a>
      </section>
    )}
  </main>

  <footer class="col">
    <p>Margaret Stewart Kennedy-Cairns · Glasgow 1929 · Montreal 2026</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/motion.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
